The powerdomain of an effectively given neighbourhood system is effectively given.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.PowerDomain`).** The powerdomain neighbourhood system has finite unions of generated upper sets as neighbourhoods and the upper set of the original master neighbourhood as master.

*Proof.* Finite unions are closed under the required intersections and every such family lies below the master family. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PowerDomain_mem`).** Membership as a powerdomain neighbourhood is exactly being a finite union of upper sets generated by original neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* This is the defining neighbourhood predicate. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PowerDomain_master`).** The master powerdomain neighbourhood is the upper set generated by the original master neighbourhood.

*Proof.* This is the defining master. ∎

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.UPX`).** For a finite list of indices, \(UPX\) is the union of the upper sets generated by the enumerated neighbourhoods at those indices.

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.UPX_def`).** \(UPX\) is the finite union of the generated upper sets named by its index list.

*Proof.* This restates the definition. ∎

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.Ypd`).** The powerdomain neighbourhood coded by \(c\) is \(UPX\) of the finite list decoded from \(c\).

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.Ypd_def`).** Decoding \(c\) and forming its finite generated union gives \(Ypd(c)\).

*Proof.* This restates the definition. ∎

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.innerInterStp`).** For a fixed outer generator, the inner intersection step examines another generator and prepends their computed intersection exactly when the pair is consistent.

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.innerInterCode`).** The inner intersection code folds the inner step through a coded finite list, beginning with a given accumulator.

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.innerInterStp_eq`).** On an explicitly packed state, the inner step either prepends the computed intersection index or leaves the accumulator unchanged, according as the consistency test returns one.

*Proof.* Unpack the state into the current generator, accumulator, and fixed generator. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.innerInterCode_eq`).** The inner code equals the corresponding left fold over the decoded list.

*Proof.* Coded iteration agrees with list iteration, and the packed step has the preceding explicit form. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.mem_UPX`).** A set belongs to \(UPX(l)\) exactly when it belongs to the upper set of some enumerated neighbourhood whose index occurs in \(l\).

*Proof.* This is membership in the finite indexed union. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.UPX_cons`).** Adding \(b\) to the front of a list adds the upper set generated by the neighbourhood indexed by \(b\) to the union.

*Proof.* Split list membership between the head and tail. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.UPX_nil`).** The union generated by the empty list is empty.

*Proof.* The empty list has no generator. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.Ypd_cons_code`).** A nonzero list code obtained by prepending \(v\) codes the union of the upper set at \(v\) with the family coded by the accumulator.

*Proof.* Decode the cons code and apply the head-tail formula for \(UPX\). ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.Ypd_zero`).** The zero code denotes the empty powerdomain neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Zero decodes to the empty list. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_exists_Ypd_aux`).** Every finite union generated by a list of original neighbourhoods is equal to \(Ypd(c)\) for some code \(c\).

*Proof.* Induct on the generator list, use surjectivity of the original presentation for the head, and prepend its index to the code for the tail. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDmem_exists_Ypd`).** Every powerdomain neighbourhood has a \(Ypd\) code.

*Proof.* Choose its finite generator list and apply the coded-list construction. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.mem_Ypd`).** A set belongs to \(Ypd(c)\) exactly when it belongs to the upper set generated by some index in the list decoded from \(c\).

*Proof.* Apply the membership characterization of \(UPX\). ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.Ypd_isPDmem`).** Every \(Ypd(c)\) is a powerdomain neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Map the decoded index list to its enumerated neighbourhoods; their finite union is exactly \(Ypd(c)\). ∎

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.outerInterStp`).** The outer intersection step runs the inner intersection code for the current generator against the second coded list.

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.interCode`).** The intersection code folds the outer step over the first coded list, starting with the empty code.

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.outerInterStp_eq`).** On an explicitly packed state, the outer step is the inner intersection code for the unpacked generator, accumulator, and second list.

*Proof.* This follows by unpacking the state. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.interCode_eq`).** The intersection code is the left fold of the inner intersection operation over the decoded first list.

*Proof.* Coded iteration agrees with list iteration and the outer step has the preceding explicit form. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.primrec_innerInterStp`).** If the consistency test is primitive recursive, then the inner intersection step is primitive recursive.

*Proof.* It is assembled from projections, pairing, the primitive-recursive intersection index, the consistency test, and numerical selection. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.primrec_outerInterStp`).** If the consistency test is primitive recursive, then the outer intersection step is primitive recursive.

*Proof.* Primitive-recursive coded folding preserves primitive recursiveness. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.primrec_interCode`).** If the consistency test is primitive recursive, then the binary intersection-code function is primitive recursive.

*Proof.* It is the coded fold of the primitive-recursive outer step. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.subCode_computable`).** It is decidable whether every generator in one decoded list is contained in some generator in another decoded list.

*Proof.* Original-neighbourhood inclusion is decidable, and bounded existential and universal search over finite decoded lists preserves decidability. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.upSet_inter_eq_empty_of_not_cons`).** If no enumerated neighbourhood lies inside the intersection of two enumerated neighbourhoods, then their generated upper sets are disjoint.

*Proof.* A member of both upper sets would be a neighbourhood inside the intersection and hence, by surjectivity, would yield a forbidden index. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.Ypd_innerstep`).** One inner step changes the coded family by adjoining the intersection of the two generated upper sets.

*Proof.* In the consistent case the computed neighbourhood equals the intersection generator; in the inconsistent case the two upper sets are disjoint and the accumulator is unchanged. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.Ypd_innerfoldl`).** Folding the inner step over a list adjoins the intersection of the fixed generator's upper set with the union generated by that list.

*Proof.* Induct on the list and distribute intersection over union. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.Ypd_innerInterCode`).** The inner intersection code denotes the old accumulator union the intersection of the fixed generator's upper set with the family denoted by the input code.

*Proof.* Replace coded iteration by its list fold and apply the inner-fold identity. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.Ypd_outerfoldl`).** Folding inner intersection over an outer list adjoins the intersection of the union generated by that list with the second coded family.

*Proof.* Induct on the outer list and distribute union over intersection. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.Ypd_interCode`).** The intersection code of \(n,m\) denotes \(Ypd(n)\cap Ypd(m)\).

*Proof.* Apply the outer-fold identity with the empty initial code. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.upSet_subset_Ypd_iff`).** The upper set generated at index \(a\) is contained in \(Ypd(k)\) exactly when some decoded generator of \(k\) contains the neighbourhood at \(a\).

*Proof.* Test inclusion on the generator itself for one direction; transitivity proves the other. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.Ypd_subset_iff`).** \(Ypd(c)\subseteq Ypd(k)\) exactly when every decoded generator of \(c\) is contained in some decoded generator of \(k\).

*Proof.* A finite union is contained in a family exactly when each summand is, then apply the generated-upper-set criterion. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.Ypd_eq_iff`).** Two coded powerdomain neighbourhoods are equal exactly when their decoded generators mutually refine one another.

*Proof.* Equality is mutual inclusion, characterized in each direction by the preceding theorem. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.eqCode_computable`).** Equality of two coded powerdomain neighbourhoods is decidable.

*Proof.* Decide the two finite mutual-refinement conditions. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.Ypd_interEq_computable`).** If consistency is primitive recursive with the stated specification, the ternary relation \(Ypd(n)\cap Ypd(m)=Ypd(k)\) is decidable.

*Proof.* Compute the intersection code and decide its equality with \(k\). ∎

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDPresentation`).** The effective powerdomain presentation enumerates \(Ypd\)-families, computes intersections by the coded double fold, and uses the singleton code of the original master index as master.

*Proof.* Coding is surjective, equality and consistency are decidable, intersection coding is primitive recursive and correct, and the chosen master code denotes the powerdomain master. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PowerDomain_isEffectivelyGiven`).** The powerdomain of an effectively given neighbourhood system is effectively given.

*Proof.* Choose an effective presentation of the original system and its computable consistency test, then form the effective powerdomain presentation. ∎
